are shown in the top-left panel of Fig. 3.18. They were used to weight the embedded parents \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons. For inclusive \(\gamma\)\(p_{T}\) spectrum, it was extracted via decaying \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons into \(\gamma\) through PYTHIA. The following algorithm was used in the PYTHIA decaying process:

* Generate Monte-Carlo \(\pi^{0}/\eta\) meson with \(\phi\) sampled from uniform distribution in \(0\sim 6.28\) range and with (\(p_{T},rapidity(y)\)) sampled from measured or calculated \(\pi^{0}/\eta\)\(dN/dp_{T}\) and \(dN/dy\) vs. \(y\) of \(\pi^{0}/\eta\) (shown in the top-right panel of Fig. 3.18).
* Simulate \(\pi^{0}/\eta{\rightarrow}e^{+}e^{-}\gamma/\gamma\gamma\) for generated mesons using PYTHIA decay routine.
* Fill \(\eta\) of generated \(\gamma\) in histogram \(hGammaEta\).
* Fill \(p_{T}\) of generated \(\gamma\) within the desired \(\Delta\eta\) acceptance in histogram \(hGammaPt\).
* Integral of \(\pi^{0}/\eta\)\(dN/dy\) gives \(N\).

Figure 3.18: Top-left panel: Invariant yield distributions of \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons. The Tsallis functions were used to fit those distributions. Top-right panel: \(dN/dy\) as a function of \(y\) for \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons. Bottom-left panel: Direct \(\gamma\) invariant yield distribution. The power-law was used to fit this distribution. Bottom-right panel: Inclusive \(\gamma\)\(p_{T}\) spectrum along with those from different sources.

* Normalize integral of histogram \(hGammaEta\) to \(N/2N\) (Dalitz decay/Two gamma decay).
* Integral of \(hGammaEta\) in the desired \(\Delta\eta\) gives \(\Delta N\).
* Normalize integral of \(hGammaPt\) to \(\Delta N\).
* Divide \(hGammaPt\) by \(\Delta\eta\) to get \(dN/dp_{T}d\eta\) of \(\gamma\) from \(\pi^{0}/\eta\).

Following the above process, the \(\gamma\) spectra from \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) meson decays were obtained. In addition, the direct \(\gamma\) contribution to inclusive \(\gamma\) need to be considered, which is from PHENIX \(p+p\) results fitted by the power-law function [103, 104, 105]. The invariant yield of direct \(\gamma\) is shown in the bottom-left panel of Fig. 3.18. The inclusive \(\gamma\)\(p_{T}\) spectrum was obtained by summing \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons decayed and direct ones, which are shown in the bottom-right panel of Fig. 3.18. The obtained \(d^{2}N/dp_{T}/dy\) distribution of \(\gamma\) was directly used to weight the embedded parents \(\gamma\). The obtained reconstruction efficiencies as a function of \(p_{T}\) for \(\gamma\) conversion, \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) Dalitz decays are shown in the right panel of Fig. 3.19.

### 2 Relative contribution

Now we need to consider relative contributions from \(\gamma\) conversion, \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) Dalitz decays to photonic electrons. The electron tracks passed the tagged electron cuts from \(\gamma\) conversion, \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) Dalitz decay embedding sample were recorded and normalized by the \(dN/dy\) of inclusive \(\gamma\), \(dN/dy\) and branch ratio of \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) mesons, respectively. The relative contributions were obtained by taking the ratio of these electrons from different sources to their summing one. The left panel of Fig. 3.19 shows the relative contributions as a function of \(p_{T}\) from different sources.

The combined reconstruction efficiency was obtained using formula 3.8. The right panel of Fig. 3.19 shows the combined reconstruction efficiency as a function of \(p_{T}\).

